<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/the-heart-and-lungs-in-exercise">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The cardiorespiratory system is <strong>the delivery engine the muscle lesson\'s factories
    depend on</strong> — and its adaptation profile (stroke volume up, capillaries up,
    threshold up) is the measurable story of getting fitter.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the equation, the adaptations, the threshold, altitude" widget="equationStep" config={cardiopulCfg} />

  <h2>Practical physiology</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Heart-rate zones, used honestly:</strong> the five-zone model works as a dial (recovery, endurance, tempo, threshold, VO₂max) — but max-HR formulas carry ±10 bpm individual error; a field test beats the formula (the testing lesson\'s individualisation).</li>
    <li><strong>Respiratory muscles fatigue too:</strong> breathing at maximum effort steals blood from the legs — inspiratory muscle training is a small real edge (the marginal-gains catalogue).</li>
    <li><strong>The athlete\'s heart vs pathology:</strong> years of endurance training remodel the heart (thicker walls, larger chambers) — distinguishing adaptation from cardiomyopathy is sports-cardiology\'s daily task (the medicine lesson\'s ECG reading, specialised).</li>
    <li><strong>Sudden cardiac death in sport:</strong> ~1 in 50,000 athletes/year, mostly undiagnosed conditions (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy) — pre-participation screening debates (ECG mandatory in Italy, debated in the US) are the stakes of the check-up lesson, competitive edition.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Levine on the athlete\'s heart; the medicine ladder\'s circulation lesson; the altitude literature (Levine & Stray-Gundersen, 1997).</p>
</TopicPage>
